--TEST--
Core\Ldap::open checks the address of a granted host, so a loopback host throws `RuntimeError` before anything is dialled — `rule:security/net-address-policy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `127.0.0.1` is granted, but it is a loopback address, and no
// program-supplied URL may reach one.
try {
    var $local = Core\Ldap::open({url: "ldaps://127.0.0.1", user: "cn=reader,dc=example,dc=test", password: "s3cret"});
    echo "opened", "\n";
} catch (Core\Ldap\LdapError $failed) {
    echo "not reached: ", $failed->message, "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `internal` written empty, as a production server has it. A CLI run reaches
# loopback unless it is written.
[capabilities.ldap]
open = ["127.0.0.1"]

[capabilities.net]
internal = []
--EXPECT--
Core\Ldap::open cannot connect to 127.0.0.1: it is loopback (127.0.0.0/8), which `net.internal` does not except
help: add `internal = ["127.0.0.1"]` under `[capabilities.net]` in nvs.toml
